---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import LegalContact from '../components/LegalContact.astro';
import { legalDetails } from '../data/legal';
const sections = [
  { id: 'scope', title: 'Scope and responsibility' },
  { id: 'website-data', title: 'Information on this website' },
  { id: 'client-data', title: 'Client project information' },
  { id: 'purposes', title: 'Purposes and lawful handling' },
  { id: 'confidentiality', title: 'Confidentiality and restricted access' },
  { id: 'training', title: 'No training with client data' },
  { id: 'security', title: 'Project security requirements' },
  { id: 'disclosure', title: 'Providers, disclosure and transfers' },
  { id: 'retention', title: 'Retention, return and deletion' },
  { id: 'incidents', title: 'Security incidents' },
  { id: 'rights', title: 'Your choices and rights' },
  { id: 'children', title: 'Children and sensitive information' },
  { id: 'changes', title: 'Changes to this policy' },
  { id: 'grievances', title: 'Privacy requests and grievances' },
];
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How SAMVIT Labs handles website information and client data, including confidentiality, restricted access, model-training restrictions and privacy requests."
  sections={sections}
  relatedHref="/terms/"
  relatedLabel="Terms of Service"
>
  <section id="scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <h2 id="scope-title">1. Scope and responsibility</h2>
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy describes the handling of personal information by{' '}
      {legalDetails.tradingName} (“SAMVIT Labs”, “we”, “us” or “our”). It
      applies to this website, communications with us, and personal information
      processed in connection with a client engagement.
    </p>
    <p>
      “Client Data” means information, documents, recordings, records,
      credentials and other material provided by or accessed on behalf of a
      client for an agreed engagement. The confidentiality and use restrictions
      below also protect Client Data that is not personal information.
    </p>
    <p>
      Indian privacy requirements apply according to their scope and
      commencement. These include the Information Technology Act, 2000 and the
      Information Technology (Reasonable Security Practices and Procedures and
      Sensitive Personal Data or Information) Rules, 2011, while applicable and
      in force. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and Digital
      Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025 have phased commencement; their
      duties and statutory remedies apply from the relevant notified dates. This
      policy does not limit mandatory rights.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="website-data" aria-labelledby="website-data-title">
    <h2 id="website-data-title">2. Information on this website</h2>
    <h3>2.1 Enquiries and demo requests</h3>
    <p>
      The forms ask for a name, work email address, company, area of interest
      and message. We use enquiry information to understand your requirements,
      respond to your request and discuss a possible engagement. Please provide
      only information needed for that conversation. Form details are not saved
      in browser storage by this website or included in the confirmation URL.
      Browser autofill and device features operate separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      Enquiries are validated through a Vercel server function, transmitted
      through Google Apps Script and recorded in Google Sheets under our
      control. Google processes this information as a service provider under its
      applicable terms. An internal email notification includes the submitted
      details. Access to enquiry records and notifications is restricted to
      authorised staff handling business communications. These records may be
      processed outside India, subject to applicable transfer requirements. They
      must be deleted when no longer needed for the enquiry or a related
      engagement, except where lawful record-keeping requires retention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Enquiry forms use Cloudflare Turnstile to check for automated abuse.
      Cloudflare may process technical information, including your IP address
      and browser characteristics, under its{' '}
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="external">
        Privacy Policy
      </a>
      . The form service also applies request limits using short-lived keyed
      hashes of network addresses and email addresses. Raw network addresses are
      not stored in the enquiry spreadsheet or its notification emails.
    </p>
    <h3>2.2 Local preferences and workflow drafts</h3>
    <p>
      The reading-theme preference is stored in this browser. In the workflow
      designer, Save stores the draft locally only when selected. Clear saved
      drafts removes those saved copies. Exported files remain on the user's
      device and must be removed separately. The designer does not upload
      drafts, sample inputs or exported files to SAMVIT Labs or run them against
      live business systems.
    </p>
    <h3>2.3 Hosting and technical information</h3>
    <p>
      Hosting and access services may process an IP address, request time,
      browser or device information, requested pages and security events to
      deliver and protect the site. The production website is hosted on Vercel.
      The hosting platform's account, authentication and infrastructure
      processing is also subject to its own applicable terms and privacy
      notices.
    </p>
    <p>
      We have not added advertising pixels, marketing cookies or third-party
      analytics scripts to the website. The hosting or authentication platform
      may use essential storage or cookies. A public topic identifier in an
      enquiry link selects a capability or workflow; it does not contain the
      visitor's form details.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="client-data" aria-labelledby="client-data-title">
    <h2 id="client-data-title">3. Client project information</h2>
    <p>
      An engagement may require business contact details, source documents,
      customer or employee records, text or audio, system identifiers,
      authorised access details and evaluation material. The relevant
      categories, individuals, purposes and systems must be identified in the
      engagement's written scope before access is granted. Only material
      necessary for that scope should be provided.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the client determines the purpose of processing and SAMVIT Labs acts
      on its behalf, SAMVIT Labs follows the client's documented, lawful
      instructions. The agreement must identify the parties' respective
      responsibilities, including notices, permissions and assistance with
      individual requests. SAMVIT Labs remains responsible for obligations that
      applicable law places directly on it. Business contact, contract and
      compliance records handled for our own purposes are considered separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      The client and its licensors retain their rights in Client Data.
      Permission to process it for a project does not transfer ownership or
      authorise unrelated use.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="purposes" aria-labelledby="purposes-title">
    <h2 id="purposes-title">4. Purposes and lawful handling</h2>
    <p>
      Information received through a business channel may be used to respond to
      the communication, assess requirements, negotiate or administer an
      engagement, deliver the agreed work, maintain necessary business records,
      protect systems and comply with law. The purpose and any necessary consent
      must be established before personal information is collected or used.
    </p>
    <p>
      A privacy notice or a visit to this website is not blanket consent for new
      processing. Where consent is required, it must be specific to the proposed
      use. A material change of purpose requires the appropriate notice,
      permission and contractual change before it takes effect. We do not sell
      personal information or Client Data.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="confidentiality" aria-labelledby="confidentiality-title">
    <h2 id="confidentiality-title">5. Confidentiality and restricted access</h2>
    <p>
      Client Data is subject to restricted staff access. Only staff authorised
      for the engagement and the task may access it. A commercial relationship
      does not authorise access to the client's other information or systems.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before any contractor or external provider receives Client Data, the
      engagement must establish its permitted role, confidentiality obligations,
      access restrictions and processing instructions. Client names, logos,
      records, recordings and project details must not be used in publicity,
      demonstrations or case studies without separate written permission.
      Confidentiality continues after the engagement, subject to lawful
      disclosure requirements.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="training" aria-labelledby="training-title">
    <h2 id="training-title">6. No training with client data</h2>
    <p class="legal-callout">
      SAMVIT Labs does not use Client Data to train or fine-tune its own or
      third-party AI models, or include it in model-training datasets. This
      prohibition also applies to the proprietary-model research programme.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using an approved model to process an input for the agreed task does not
      authorise training, unrelated evaluation or reuse of the input. Any
      processing provider proposed for an engagement must be assessed for these
      restrictions; its permitted use, retention and model-training terms must
      be agreed before Client Data is sent to it. Client Data must not be
      entered into an unapproved public AI account or training-enabled service.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
    <h2 id="security-title">7. Project security requirements</h2>
    <p>
      Restricted staff access and the prohibition on training with Client Data
      are current policies. Additional technical safeguards depend on the system
      and must be documented and verified before the relevant project data is
      processed. The security schedule must address:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Authorised users, access permissions, privileged accounts,
        authentication requirements and removal of access.
      </li>
      <li>
        Encryption in transit and at rest, key management, and any limitations
        of the selected infrastructure.
      </li>
      <li>
        Separation of client environments, approved devices, credential handling
        and secure transfer methods.
      </li>
      <li>
        Logging, security updates, vulnerability handling, backups, recovery and
        incident responsibilities.
      </li>
      <li>
        Retention periods, deletion of working copies and treatment of backups
        and legally required records.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A control that is required by applicable law cannot be waived by a project
      agreement. This policy does not represent that every proposed control is
      already implemented in every environment, or that SAMVIT Labs holds an
      independent security certification. No system can guarantee absolute
      security.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="disclosure" aria-labelledby="disclosure-title">
    <h2 id="disclosure-title">8. Providers, disclosure and transfers</h2>
    <p>
      Project access by hosting, model or support providers must be limited to
      the agreed purpose and covered by appropriate written restrictions. The
      client must be informed of the providers and material processing locations
      proposed for its data. A provider change or transfer must follow the
      agreed authorisation process and applicable law.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cross-border processing requires the applicable legal conditions, any
      required permission, appropriate protection and consideration of
      sector-specific localisation requirements. SAMVIT Labs does not promise
      that all processing occurs in India or that a self-hosted system excludes
      all third-party access. Those matters must be established for the actual
      deployment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Information may also be disclosed where a valid law, court order or
      authorised government request requires it. Disclosure must be limited to
      what is legally required. Where permitted, affected clients will be
      informed of a demand concerning their data.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="retention" aria-labelledby="retention-title">
    <h2 id="retention-title">9. Retention, return and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Client Data must be retained only for the agreed purpose and period, or as
      required by law. Before an engagement begins, the parties must specify a
      retention schedule and the process for return or deletion on completion or
      termination. Any backup-deletion cycle and legally required exceptions
      must be explained; retained copies must remain restricted and unavailable
      for unrelated use.
    </p>
    <p>
      Business correspondence, contracts and compliance records may need a
      different retention period from project working files. Where CERT-In
      directions apply, required ICT logs must be retained for the prescribed
      period, including the applicable 180-day minimum and Indian-jurisdiction
      requirement. This is not a blanket retention period for all Client Data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Local browser preferences and saved drafts remain until removed through
      the relevant controls or browser settings. SAMVIT Labs cannot remotely
      erase files exported to a user's device.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="incidents" aria-labelledby="incidents-title">
    <h2 id="incidents-title">10. Security incidents</h2>
    <p>
      Project agreements must identify the contacts and procedures for
      containment, investigation, preservation of necessary evidence and
      restoration. SAMVIT Labs will notify an affected client without undue
      delay after becoming aware of a personal-data breach affecting its
      project, with available information and subsequent material updates,
      subject to lawful restrictions and any stricter applicable deadline.
    </p>
    <p>
      Statutory reports must be made within the relevant legal time limits.
      Where the CERT-In directions apply, listed cyber incidents must be
      reported to CERT-In within six hours of noticing them or being informed of
      them. This authority-reporting requirement is separate from client
      notification. Any duties to notify affected individuals or the Data
      Protection Board apply according to the relevant law and its commencement.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="rights" aria-labelledby="rights-title">
    <h2 id="rights-title">11. Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
      You may request information about the personal information SAMVIT Labs
      holds about you, correction of inaccurate details, withdrawal of consent
      where consent is the basis of processing, or deletion where there is no
      lawful reason to retain it. The request will be assessed under applicable
      law and the relevant processing role. Where SAMVIT Labs acts for a client,
      it may need to refer the request to that client and assist it rather than
      disclose the client's records directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      We may ask for proportionate information to verify identity and authority.
      Withdrawal applies to future consent-based processing and may affect a
      service that needs the information; it does not remove lawful retention
      duties. We will explain any restriction or refusal that the law permits us
      to explain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rights under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, including its
      specific access, correction, erasure, grievance and nomination mechanisms,
      apply when the relevant provisions commence and the processing is within
      scope. Nothing here excludes a statutory complaint or remedy.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="children" aria-labelledby="children-title">
    <h2 id="children-title">12. Children and sensitive information</h2>
    <p>
      This website and its enquiry process are intended for business users, not
      children. Do not put passwords, financial account details, health records,
      biometric information or children's information into website examples or
      enquiry forms. Processing such information in a client engagement requires
      a separately agreed lawful purpose, required permissions and safeguards
      appropriate to the information.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="changes" aria-labelledby="changes-title">
    <h2 id="changes-title">13. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      Revisions will identify the date and version of the updated policy.
      Material changes to project processing must also follow the client's
      agreement and any required notice or consent procedure. Updating this page
      does not retrospectively authorise a new use of Client Data.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="grievances" aria-labelledby="grievances-title">
    <h2 id="grievances-title">14. Privacy requests and grievances</h2>
    <p>
      Send a privacy request or grievance using the contact details below.
      Describe the matter and how you can be contacted; avoid sending
      unnecessary sensitive records. Where the 2011 SPDI Rules apply, grievances
      must be addressed expeditiously and within one month of receipt, or within
      a shorter period required by another applicable rule.
    </p>
    <LegalContact privacy />
    <p>
      Clearly identify privacy requests so they can be directed to the person
      responsible for data protection. An enquiry confirmation does not replace
      a substantive response to a privacy request.
    </p>
  </section>
</Legal>
